matrix}5 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe other options will not give us a matrix in row echelon form."] [17592186340090 :answer/value "${a}_{14}$ and ${a}_{41}$ only"] [17592186340486 :answer/value "${a}_{33}$ only"] [17592186340851 :answer/value "${a}_{23}$ and ${a}_{33}$ only"] [17592186340918 :answer/value "${a}_{41}$ only"] [17592186340964 :answer/value "${a}_{33}$ and ${a}_{41}$ only"] [17592186402803 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 7 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186402803 :question/worked-solution "Notice that by replacing ${a}_{31}$ and ${a}_{41}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 7 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟶\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe other options will not give us a matrix in row echelon form."] [17592186402805 :answer/value "${a}_{22}$ and ${a}_{31}$ only"] [17592186402806 :answer/value "${a}_{31}$ and ${a}_{41}$ only"] [17592186402807 :answer/value "${a}_{41}$ only"] [17592186402808 :answer/value "${a}_{22}$ and ${a}_{41}$ only"] [17592186402809 :answer/value "${a}_{11}$ and ${a}_{22}$ only"] [17592186413822 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186413822 :question/worked-solution "Notice that by replacing ${a}_{32}$, ${a}_{43}$ and ${a}_{44}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟶\\,\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe other options will not give us a matrix in row echelon form."] [17592186413799 :answer/value "${a}_{43}$ only"] [17592186413804 :answer/value "${a}_{32}$, ${a}_{43}$ and ${a}_{44}$ only"] [17592186413808 :answer/value "${a}_{32}$ only"] [17592186413809 :answer/value "${a}_{32}$ and ${a}_{22}$ only"] [17592186413815 :answer/value "${a}_{32}$ and ${a}_{43}$ only"] [17592186130380 :tutorial/content "Let's consider our matrices $A$ and $B$ once more, with the leading entries highlighted.\n\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} & 6 & 7 & 8 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} & 6 & 9 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{red}1}} & 4 & 7 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{5} & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB = \\begin{bmatrix}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & 5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} & 4 & 3 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-2} & 1 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe say that a matrix is in **row echelon form** if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nHere, $B$ is in row echelon form since both conditions are satisfied. However, $A$ is not in row echelon form since condition II is violated: the leading entry of the third row $(a_{32}={\\color{red}1})$ is placed just below the leading entry of the second row $(a_{22}=3).$\n\nGenerally speaking, row echelon form means that we have *stairs* where each step (represented by a leading entry $\\bbox[lightgray, 3pt]{\\large\\ast}$) has a height of $1.$\n\n![](images/67/67f83c454e59d16ae80cf67a19b19f968182251a2e5d1b405e2c422e0313d5b7.png)\n\nFinally, an *augmented* matrix is in row echelon form if the coefficient matrix (i.e., the part that is to the left of the vertical bar) is in row echelon form."] [17592186128995 :question/problem "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $1$st, $3$rd and $4$th rows in the matrix $A,$ given below?\n\n$$\nA=\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n"] [17592186128995 :question/worked-solution "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry. \n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $1$st, $3$rd and $4$th rows of $A$ are $-1$, $-4$ and $8$, respectively.\n\n$$\nA\n=\\begin{bmatrix}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-1} & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-4} & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{8} & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is $-1+(-4)+8=3.$"] [17592186129776 :question/problem "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $2$nd, $3$rd, and $4$th rows in the matrix $A$, given below?\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186128138 :answer/value "7"] [17592186128139 :answer/value "6"] [17592186128140 :answer/value "8"] [17592186128141 :answer/value "5"] [17592186128142 :answer/value "10"] [17592186129777 :question/problem "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $1$st and $2$nd rows in the matrix $A$, given below?\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186128144 :answer/value "4"] [17592186128145 :answer/value "2"] [17592186128146 :answer/value "1"] [17592186128147 :answer/value "-2"] [17592186128148 :answer/value "-1"] [17592186262981 :question/problem "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd rows in the matrix $A,$ given below?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186262981 :question/worked-solution "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry. \n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd rows of $A$ are $-3$, $5$, and $2$, respectively.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is $-3+5+2=4.$"] [17592186260411 :answer/value "-2"] [17592186260488 :answer/value "9"] [17592186260677 :answer/value "4"] [17592186261202 :answer/value "6"] [17592186261771 :answer/value "1"] [17592186262982 :question/problem "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $1$st and $4$th rows in the matrix $A,$ given below?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 9 & -8 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186262982 :question/worked-solution "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry. \n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $1$st and $4$th rows of  $A$ are $-4$ and $9$, respectively.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 9 & -8 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is $-4+9=5.$"] [17592186259919 :answer/value "-5"] [17592186260477 :answer/value "-1"] [17592186260818 :answer/value "-13"] [17592186261046 :answer/value "4"] [17592186261799 :answer/value "5"] [17592186262984 :question/problem "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $2$nd and $4$th rows in the matrix $A,$ given below?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186262984 :question/worked-solution "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry. \n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $2$nd and $4$th rows of  $A$ are $