\n{x}_{1}-2(4)+3(-1)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=8+3=11.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=11$, ${x}_{2}=4$, and ${x}_{3}=-1.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨11,4,-1⟩.$"] [17592186264096 :answer/value "⟨-5,-4,-1⟩"] [17592186264405 :answer/value "⟨11,2,-1⟩"] [17592186264444 :answer/value "⟨11,4,-1⟩"] [17592186264528 :answer/value "⟨7,2,-1⟩"] [17592186264910 :answer/value "⟨7,-4,-1⟩"] [17592186265750 :question/problem "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186265750 :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x$, $y$, and $z.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+2z &=-1 \\\\ y+3z &=2 \\\\ z &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that $z=3.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+3(3)=2\\,⟹\\,y=2-9=-7.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=3$ and $y=-7$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx+(-7)+2(3)=-1\\,⟹\\,x=-1+7-6=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=0$, $y=-7$, and $z=3.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨0,-7,3⟩.$"] [17592186263750 :answer/value "⟨-14,7,3⟩"] [17592186264124 :answer/value "⟨0,7,3⟩"] [17592186264225 :answer/value "⟨4,-11,3⟩"] [17592186264365 :answer/value "⟨-14,-7,3⟩"] [17592186265030 :answer/value "⟨0,-7,3⟩"] [17592186287990 :question/problem "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186287990 :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say,  ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=-1 \\\\ {x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=-2 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that  ${x}_{3}=-3.$ Substituting this into the second equation,  we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+2(-3)=-2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-2-2(-3)=-2+6=4.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=-3$ and ${x}_{2}=4$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(4)+3(-3)=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-1-8+9=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=0$, ${x}_{2}=4$, and ${x}_{3}=-3.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186285617 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186286009 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186286231 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186286438 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186286929 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186341889 :question/problem "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186341889 :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x$, $y$, and $z.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+z &=-8 \\\\ y+2z &=-1 \\\\ z &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that $z=2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+2(2)=-1\\,⟹\\,y=-1-4=-5.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=2$ and $y=-5$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx+2(-5)+(2)=-8\\,⟹\\,x=-8+10-2=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=0$, $y=-5$, and $z=2.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨0,-5,2⟩.$"] [17592186339340 :answer/value "⟨-2,-4,2⟩"] [17592186340374 :answer/value "⟨0,-5,2⟩"] [17592186340800 :answer/value "⟨0,-8,0⟩"] [17592186340981 :answer/value "⟨1,-3,-3⟩"] [17592186341101 :answer/value "⟨-2,-1,-4⟩"] [17592186341890 :question/problem "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186341890 :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say,  ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=-3 \\\\ {x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=-4 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that  ${x}_{3}=-1.$ Substituting this into the second equation,  we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+3(-1)=-4\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-4+3=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=-1$ and ${x}_{2}=-1$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(-1)+4(-1)=-3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-3+1+4=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=2$, ${x}_{2}=-1$, and ${x}_{3}=-1.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186339730 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186339973 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186339974 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186340520 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186340665 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186096458 :question/problem "Solve the system of equations that is represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 3\\end{array} \n\t\\right].\n$$\n\n"] [17592186096458 :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x_1,$ $x_2,$ and $x_3.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-2 \\\\ 3{x}_{3} &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that\n\n$$\n3x_3=3 \n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \n\tx_3=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2+(1)=-2\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\tx_2=-2-1=-3.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute both $x_3 = 1$ and $x_2 = -3$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n2x_1+(-3)-(1) = 0\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\tx_1 = \\dfrac{1}{2} (3 + 1) = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x_1 = 2,$ $x_2=-3,$ and $x_3 = 1.$ Or, in vector form, the solution is $\\langle 2,-3,1 \\rangle.$"] [17592186096923 :question/problem "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix:\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$."] [17592186093513 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186093514 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186093515 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186093516 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186093517 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186096924 :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y-z &=10 \\\\ y+5z &=15 \\\\ -z &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186096924 :question/worked-solution "The third equation states that \n\n$$\n-z=5\\,⟹\\,z=-5.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+5(-5)=15\\,⟹\\,y=15+25=40.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=-5$ and $y=40$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx+2(40)-(-5)=10\\,⟹\\,x=10-80-5=-75.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-75,y=40$ and $z=-5.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨-75,40,-5⟩.$"] [17592186236335 :answer/value "⟨-15,40,-5⟩"] [17592186236375 :answer/value "⟨-85,40,-5⟩"] [17592186236570 :answer/value "⟨-75,40,-5⟩"] [17592186236790 :answer/value "⟨-5,-10,5⟩"] [17592186236981 :answer/value "⟨-75,-10,-5⟩"] [17592186237870 :question/problem "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186237870 :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x$, $y$, and $z.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-2y+z &=8 \\\\ y-z &=-1 \\\\ z &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that $z=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny-0=-1\\,⟹\\,y=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=0$ and $y=-1$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n2x-2(-1)+(0)=8\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{8-2}{2}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=3$, $y=-1$, and $z=0.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨3,-1,0⟩.$"] [17592186236428 :answer/value "⟨2,-1,0⟩"] [17592186236657 :answer/value "⟨0,-1,8⟩"] [17592186236791 :answer/value "⟨2,-2,0⟩"] [17592186236847 :answer/value "⟨0,1,-8⟩"] [17592186237056 :answer/value "⟨3,-1,0⟩"] [17592186265751 :question/problem "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix: \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186265751 :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x$, $y$, and $z.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+y+2z &=-3 \\\\ y+3z &=0 \\\\ -z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that \n\n$$\n-z=1\\,⟹\\,z=-1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+3(-1)=0\\,⟹\\,y=3.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=-1$ and $y=3$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n4x+(3)+2(-1)=-3\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{-3-3+2}{4}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-1$, $y=3$, and $z=-1.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as \n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186263410 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}."] [17592186263903 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}."] [17592186263938 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186265105 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}."] [17592186265115 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}."] [17592186265752 :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=-1 \\\\ {x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=-3 \\\\ -{x}_{3} &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186265752 :question/worked-solution "The third equation states that \n\n$$\n-{x}_{3}=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{3}=1.\n$$\n\n Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+2(1)=-3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-3-2=-5.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=1$ and ${x}_{2}=-5$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}-(-5)+4(1)=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{-1-5-4}{2}=-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is \n${x}_{1}=-5$, ${x}_{2}=-5$, and ${x}_{3}=1.$  In vector form, the solution can be written as $$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186263151 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186263351 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186263853 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186264309 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186264632 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186265753 :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-1 \\\\ -{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=7 \\\\ 2{x}_{3} &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186265753 :question/worked-solution "The third equation states that \n\n$$\n2{x}_{3}=4\\,⟹\\,{x}_{3}=2.\n$$\n\n Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-{x}_{2}+3(2)=7\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=6-7=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=-1$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+5(-1)+(2)=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-1+5-2=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is \n${x}_{1}=2$, ${x}_{2}=-1$, and ${x}_{3}=2.$  In vector form, the solution can be written as $$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186263706 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186264015 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186264025 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186264319 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-30 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186264748 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186341891 :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y-z &=2 \\\\ y+3z &=6 \\\\ -z &=-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186341891 :question/worked-solution "The third equation states that\n\n$$\n-z=-3\\,⟹\\,z=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+3(3)=6\\,⟹\\,y=6-9=-3.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=3$ and $y=-3$ into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n2x+(-3)-(3)=2\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}(2+3+3)=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=4$, $y=-3$ and $z=3.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨4,-3,3⟩.$"] [17592186339143 :answer/value "⟨1,3,-3⟩"] [17592186339815 :answer/value "⟨2,-4,3⟩"] [17592186340578 :answer/value "⟨3,4,-3⟩"] [17592186341106 :answer/value "⟨1,-3,3⟩"] [17592186341119 :answer/value "⟨4,-3,3⟩"] [17592186341892 :question/problem "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix: \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186341892 :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x$, $y$, and $z.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+z &=2 \\\\ 3y+2z &=-1 \\\\ -z &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that\n\n$$\n-z=2\\,⟹\\,z=-2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n3y+2(-2)=-1\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{3}(-1+4)=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=-2$ and $y=1$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx+(-2)=2\\,⟹\\,x=2+2=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=4$, $y=1$, and $z=-2.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as \n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186339326 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186340049 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186340833 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186341099 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186341217 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186341893 :question/problem "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186341893 :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x$, $y$, and $z.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y+z &=-1 \\\\ y+z &=1 \\\\ 2z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that\n \n\n$$\n2z=1\\,⟹\\,z=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+(\\frac{1}{2})=1\\,⟹\\,y=1-\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=y=\\frac{1}{2}$  into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n2x+(\\frac{1}{2})+(\\frac{1}{2})=-1\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}(-1-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-1$, $y=\\frac{1}{2}$, and $z=\\frac{1}{2}.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨-1,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩.$"] [17592186339616 :answer/value "⟨1,\\frac{1}{2},1⟩"] [17592186339702 :answer/value "⟨-1,-1,\\frac{1}{2}⟩"] [17592186339946 :answer/value "⟨-1,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩"] [17592186340272 :answer/value "⟨-1,\\frac{1}{2},-1⟩"] [17592186340752 :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2},-1⟩"] [17592186163179 :tutorial/content "We can do the same trick using the **elementary column operations** instead of row operations.\n\nThere are three types of elementary *column* operations, which are entirely analogous to the corresponding row operations:\n\n- Switching any two columns: \n\n$$\nC_i \\leftrightarrow C_j\n$$\n\n- Multiplying (each element of) a column by a non-zero number: \n\n$$\nC_i := k C_i,\\qquad k \\neq 0\n$$\n\n- Adding a multiple of one column to another column: \n\n$$\nC_j := C_j+k C_i,\\qquad j \\neq i\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** \n\n- Unlike elementary row operations, elementary *column* operations change the solutions of a system of linear equations. This is because column operations alter the coefficients associated with each variable across the system, changing the equations.\n\n- The exception is swapping two columns - this doesn't alter the solutions *provided that* we swap the corresponding variables in the solution vector. For example, if we swap the first and second columns, we must treat the second variable as the first and vice versa. The solution set remains unchanged because the relationships between the variables and their coefficients are preserved."] [17592186163214 :tutorial/content "When studying linear algebra, it is common to use elementary row operations to reduce a matrix $A$ to another matrix $B$ such that $B$ contains many zeros. \n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -5 & -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the entry $a_{11}$ located in the top left-hand corner. We can reduce *all* of the entries *below* it using elementary row operations.\n\nFirst, we subtract $3$ times the first row from the second row.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A= & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -5 & -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}: &={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 10 \\\\ -5 & -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we add $5$ times the first row to the third row.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 10 \\\\ -5 & -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}: &={R}_{3}+5{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -14\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done! We have successfully performed elementary row operations to reduce all of the entries below $a_{11}$ to zero."] [17592186161757 :question/problem "\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ {\\color{blue}1} & \\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{{\\color{blue}1}} & {\\color{blue}2} & {\\color{blue}-1} \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & {\\color{red}0} & -3 & 7 \\\\ {\\color{blue}1} & \\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{{\\color{blue}1}} & {\\color{blue}2} & {\\color{blue}-1} \\\\ -4 & {\\color{red}0} & -5 & 4 \\\\ 2 & {\\color{red}0} & 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary row operations \n\n- $R_1 := \\ldots$\n\n- $R_3:=R_3-3R_2$\n\n- $R_4:= \\ldots$\n\neach of the form $R_i:=R_i+kR_2,$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the missing elementary row operations?"] [17592186161757 :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries above and below $a_{22}={\\color{blue}\\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{1}}$ equal to ${\\color{red}0}.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}: &={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}: &={R}_{3}-3{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{4}: &={R}_{4}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing elementary row operations are\n\n$\\qquad$ $R_1 := R_1 - 2R_2\\quad$ and $\\quad R_4:=R_4+2R_2.$"] [17592186162554 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}13 & 0 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 20 & 0 & 19 & 2 \\\\ 25 & 0 & 19 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary row operations \n\n- ${R}_{1}:=…$\n- ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2}$\n- ${R}_{4}:=…$\n\neach of the form ${R}_{i}:={R}_{i}+k{R}_{2},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the missing elementary row operations?"] [17592186162554 :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries above and below ${a}_{22}=-1$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}: &={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}13 & 0 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}: &={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}13 & 0 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 20 & 0 & 19 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{4}: &={R}_{4}+5{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}13 & 0 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 20 & 0 & 19 & 2 \\\\ 25 & 0 & 19 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing elementary row operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+5{R}_{2}.$"] [17592186326991 :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-4{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}-5{R}_{2}$"] [17592186327324 :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-3{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}-5{R}_{2}$"] [17592186327958 :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+5{R}_{2}$"] [17592186328739 :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+4{R}_{2}$"] [17592186329114 :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}-4{R}_{2}$"] [17592186162571 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -7 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 10 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 8 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -7 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 13 & 14 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -14 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary row operations \n\n- ${R}_{2}:=…$\n- ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}$\n- ${R}_{4}:=…$\n\neach of the form ${R}_{i}:={R}_{i}+k{R}_{1},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the missing elementary row operations?"] [17592186162571 :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries below ${a}_{11}=1$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -7 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 10 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 8 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}: &={R}_{2}-{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -7 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 13 & 14 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 8 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}: &={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -7 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 13 & 14 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{4}: &={R}_{4}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -7 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 13 & 14 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -14 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the missing elementary row operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+2{R}_{1}.$"] [17592186412015 :answer/value "${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+2{R}_{1}$"] [17592186412016 :answer/value "${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}-2{R}_{1}$"] [17592186412019 :answer/value "${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{1}$"] [17592186412022 :answer/value "${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}-2{R}_{1}$"] [17592186412023 :answer/value "${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+2{R}_{1}$"] [17592186265911 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 & 11 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 9 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -7 & -27 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 7 & 18 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary row operations \n\n- ${R}_{1}:=…$\n- ${R}_{2}:=…$\n- ${R}_{4}:={R}_{4}-{R}_{3}$\n\neach of the form ${R}_{i}:={R}_{i}+k{R}_{3},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the missing elementary row operations?"] [17592186265911 :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries above and below ${a}_{32}=2$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 & 11 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 9 & 8\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}: &={R}_{1}-3{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -7 & -27 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 9 & 8\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}: &={R}_{2}+2{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -7 & -27 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 7 & 18 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 9 & 8\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{4}: &={R}_{4}-{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -7 & -27 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 7 & 18 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the missing elementary row operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-3{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{3}.$"] [17592186263227 :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{3}$"] [17592186263425 :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{3}$"] [17592186264017 :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-3{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{3}$"] [17592186264154 :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{3}$"] [17592186265063 :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-3{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{3}$"] [17592186265912 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-17 & 8 & 0 & 7 \\\\ -7 & 4 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 7 & 3 \\\\ -7 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary row operations \n\n- ${R}_{1}:=…$\n- ${R}_{2}:=…$\n- ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-2{R}_{4}$\n\neach of the form ${R}_{i}:={R}_{i}+k{R}_{4},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the missing elementary row operations?"] [17592186265912 :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries above ${a}_{42}=-2$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-17 & 8 & 0 & 7 \\\\ -7 & 4 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}: &={R}_{1}+4{R}_{4}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -7 & 4 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}: &={R}_